  "live_records": {
    "q-215722": {
      "dom_id": "step-q215722",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "The lines $y=\\frac{2}{3}x+4$ and $y=mx+2$ are parallel.  What is the value of $m?$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nBoth lines are in slope-intercept form, so we can pick out the slope of each equation by looking at the $x$-coefficient.\n\n- The slope of the line $y=\\frac{2}{3}x+4$ is $\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\n- The slope of the line $y=mx+2$ is $m.$\n\nBecause the slopes must be the same, we must have $m=\\frac{2}{3}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
