    "q-28288": {
      "dom_id": "step-q28288",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "A line $l$ is parallel to $4x-2y=6.$ A possible equation for $l$ is",
      "worked_solution": "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nTo find the slope of the line $4x-2y=6,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-2y & =6 \\\\ -2y & =-4x+6 \\\\ y & =2x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $2.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only line that has a slope of $2$ is $y=2x+6.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
