    "q-110095": {
      "dom_id": "step-q110095",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "The lines $y=-3x+1$ and $kx-2y=12$ are parallel. The value of $k=$",
      "worked_solution": "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\n- The line $y=-3x+1$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $-3.$\n\n- To find the slope of the line $kx-2y=12,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kx-2y & =12 \\\\ -2y & =-kx+12 \\\\ 2y & =kx-12 \\\\ y & =\\frac{k}{2}x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $\\frac{k}{2}.$\n\nSince the slopes of the two lines must be equal, we must have $\\frac{k}{2}=-3.$ Consequently, $k=-6.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
